<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  JOY CREATOR'S DISPLAY FACE — Archivo.

  This is the face the lobby was mocked up in, and it is here because JoyRaptor
  said "Make sure for the lobby you use the font you actually had for the mockup.
  Don't cheap out. You make it look good. Stock android is boring."

  He is right, and the reason is structural rather than a matter of taste.
  Roboto (which is what sans-serif-black actually gives you) is the system voice.
  Every notification, every settings screen, every other app on the phone speaks
  in it. A product that wants to be a place rather than a utility cannot share
  its voice with the operating system.

  Archivo is a grotesque with very tight apertures and a near-flat top to the
  lowercase 'a' and 's'. At display weights it goes DENSE rather than merely
  large, which is what makes a short word like STUDIO hold a whole hero without
  needing to be enormous.

  ── WHY ONE FILE AND SIX ENTRIES ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  archivo_variable.ttf is a VARIABLE font: a single outline with a 'wght' axis
  that can be interpolated anywhere from 100 to 900. Declaring each weight as its
  own <font> entry pointing at the same file, differing only in the variation
  setting, is how Android exposes that axis to android:textFontWeight and to
  Typeface.create(family, weight, italic).

  fontVariationSettings landed in API 26. minSdk here is 24, so on Android 7.0
  and 7.1 the axis is ignored and every weight renders at Archivo Regular. That
  is a graceful fall: still Archivo, still not the system voice, just flatter.
  It is not worth shipping six static files to fix two API levels.
-->
<font-family xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto">
    <font app:fontStyle="normal" app:fontWeight="400"
          app:font="@font/archivo_variable" app:fontVariationSettings="'wght' 400" />
    <font app:fontStyle="normal" app:fontWeight="500"
          app:font="@font/archivo_variable" app:fontVariationSettings="'wght' 500" />
    <font app:fontStyle="normal" app:fontWeight="600"
          app:font="@font/archivo_variable" app:fontVariationSettings="'wght' 600" />
    <font app:fontStyle="normal" app:fontWeight="700"
          app:font="@font/archivo_variable" app:fontVariationSettings="'wght' 700" />
    <font app:fontStyle="normal" app:fontWeight="800"
          app:font="@font/archivo_variable" app:fontVariationSettings="'wght' 800" />
    <font app:fontStyle="normal" app:fontWeight="900"
          app:font="@font/archivo_variable" app:fontVariationSettings="'wght' 900" />
</font-family>
